# Summary of changes for run 07561493-f6f9-4377-b690-1c729aec42b1
I wrote all five deliverables for the "Five Fields, One Law" package and committed them to the project root. The `.tex` file has not been compiled, because `pdflatex` isn't available here, so it needs a test build. The three plotting scripts have also never been run (`matplotlib` isn't installed); I only checked that they parse.

- **`ARTICLE.md`** (about 2,300 words): a popular-science telling of the result. It explains:
  - the splitting type of \(x^3-4x+1\) modulo a prime;
  - the sign of the Frobenius element (the permutation of the three roots that each prime picks out), presented as a hidden fair coin;
  - the Splitting-Type Law: exactly one root mod \(p\) if and only if \(p\) is a non-residue mod 229, with an elementary proof sketch;
  - the exact one-bit value, split as \(H(T)=\tfrac23+\tfrac12\log_2 3\) and \(H(T\mid\text{class})=\tfrac12\log_2 3-\tfrac13\);
  - the witness pair 3 and 461;
  - the Fibre-Product Law, which explains why every field gives one bit;
  - why the reported 1.0078 is finite-sample bias, not signal.
- **`RESEARCH_PAPER.md`** (about 4,300 words) and **`RESEARCH_PAPER.tex`** (amsart, mirrors the Markdown version). Every result is stated in full with a proof sketch:
  - the Fibre-Product Law \(I=\log_2|\Delta|\), marginal invariance, group and trivial-quotient corollaries;
  - the one-bit law for every \(S_n\) with \(n\ge2\) and for every odd prime conductor;
  - for \(x^3-4x+1\): the discriminant identity \(\delta^2=229\), the residual identity, Stickelberger by an explicit Frobenius computation, the second-root lemma, and the Splitting-Type Law in both forms;
  - "never two roots", the residue branch and the witness pair;
  - irreducibility over \(\mathbb Q\), Galois group \(S_3\), and three real roots.

  It also covers algorithms, numerical illustrations, discussion and future work. None of the three prose documents mentions the formal development or any file.
- **`demo.py`** (standard library only, runs in about one second). On all 17,982 odd primes up to 200,000 it finds no exception to the Splitting-Type Law and no prime with two roots. The exact model values come out as 1 bit for \(S_2\) through \(S_5\), 0.656 bits for the \(S_4\) fixed-point count (where the law's hypothesis fails), and 0 bits for the independent product. The plug-in estimate of the information falls from 1.074 at 1,000 primes to 1.0023 at 17,982. These are numerical illustrations, not part of the proofs.
- **`PACKAGE.json`**:
  - **Metadata:** domain "Bridges", because the concept's "Combinatorics" is not an allowed value and the work links number theory with information theory. It also has five key results and eight keywords.
  - **Embedded content:** the full article, paper, LaTeX and demo, plus four algorithms with pseudocode and tested code, three plotting scripts, and two interactive HTML widgets. The JavaScript in both widgets passes a syntax check, but I have not tested them in a browser.
  - **Guided page:** a narrative that places the elements inline and hides the proofs behind expandable sections.
  - **Future directions:** the Phase A text, lightly edited. I completed its cut-off final sentence. I also added a note that the numerics put the bias constant at roughly half the conjectured coefficient. Separately, the source text's prediction \(N\approx10^4\) doesn't match the demo: 1.0078 falls between the 5,000-prime and 10,000-prime estimates (1.0106 and 1.0045).
  - **Formal source:** both Phase A source files in full, with their paths listed.

I left one claim from the Phase A comments out of the prose. It cites cyclic cubic fields as an example of the zero-bit (trivial-quotient) case, but for those fields the residue class actually determines the splitting type completely, so I used plain independence as the zero-bit example instead. One of the two Phase A files imports a shared module that isn't in this project; the package lists only the two files that exist.
